{"patch":{"config":{"title":"Antranig Basman","baseURL":"https://ponder.org.uk","defaultContentLanguage":"en","mainSections":["post","posts"],"author":{"name":"Antranig Basman","website":"/","email":"amb26web@ponder.org.uk","twitter":"amb26ponder"},"params":{"homeTitle":"Antranig Basman","subtitle":"Building habitable technologies","bio":"img/20230209_135356 - bio - cropped.jpg","logo":"img/pond.jpg","favicon":"img/favicon.ico","dateFormat":"January 2, 2006","comments":true,"socialShare":true,"showRelatedPosts":true,"commentsLayer":"beautifulWend.comments.giscus","customCSS":["css/ponder.css"],"mediaIcons":[{"title":"Twitter profile","href":"http://twitter.com/amb26ponder","icon":"fab fa-twitter"},{"title":"Send me a message","href":"mailto:amb26webpage@ponder.org.uk","icon":"fa fa-envelope"},{"title":"Antranig's GitHub profile","href":"http://github.com/amb26","icon":"fab fa-github-alt"},{"title":"My papers on DBLP","href":"https://dblp.org/pid/68/5013.html","icon":"fa fa-university"},{"title":"My LinkedIn profile","href":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/antranigbasman/","icon":"fab fa-linkedin"}],"giscus":{"repo":"amb26/ponder","repoId":"R_kgDOOKllGA","category":"Announcements","categoryId":"DIC_kwDOOKllGM4CvhAS"}},"menu":{"main":[{"name":"Posts","url":"post","weight":1},{"name":"WiP","url":"wip","weight":1},{"name":"Docs","identifier":"docs","url":"docs","weight":3},{"name":"Fluid Signals","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-signals","weight":1},{"name":"Fluid Integration Language","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-integration-language","weight":2},{"name":"Fluid View","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-view","weight":3},{"name":"Fluid PTF","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-ptf","weight":4},{"name":"Fluid tldraw integration","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-tldraw","weight":5},{"name":"wend","parent":"docs","url":"docs/wend","weight":6},{"name":"About","url":"page/about/","weight":3},{"name":"Communities","url":"communities","weight":4},{"name":"Projects","url":"projects","weight":5},{"name":"Terms","url":"term","weight":5}]}},"features":{"selfHosted":true,"hljs":true}},"pages":[{"kind":"page","type":"wip","section":"wip","title":"Hunting rich editors","date":"2025-03-28T00:00:00.000Z","relPermalink":"/wip/2025-03-28-hunting-rich-editors/","permalink":"https://ponder.org.uk/wip/2025-03-28-hunting-rich-editors/","outputPath":"/wip/2025-03-28-hunting-rich-editors/index.html","params":{"title":"Hunting rich editors","date":"2025-03-28"},"sourcePath":"content/wip/2025-03-28-hunting-rich-editors.md","content":"<p>The substrate’s transition to editability will need to present a “mostly rendered” view of itself – managed\nby some form of rich text editor. A huge “grab-bag list” is at JefMari’s curated list of\n<a href=\"https://github.com/JefMari/awesome-wysiwyg-editors\">Awesome Wysiwyg Editors</a> which can rapidly be whittled down\nto a shortlist. <a href=\"https://github.com/ckeditor/ckeditor5\">CKEditor 5</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/tinymce/tinymce\">TinyMCE</a>\nare familiar from decades ago. But the gold standard is clearly Marijn’s <a href=\"https://prosemirror.net/\">ProseMirror</a> –\n<a href=\"https://prosemirror.net/examples/basic/\">basic example</a> here and really nice markdown editing example here.</p>\n<p>It feels as if an Infusion component tree could very easily be the backing of one of Marijn’s <a href=\"https://prosemirror.net/docs/guide/#doc\">document models</a>\nbut we’d need to produce a <a href=\"https://prosemirror.net/docs/guide/#schema\">schema</a>. Indeed the whole thing is feeling\nrather heavyweight and not the kind of thing we could bash into a demo in a couple of weeks. A checkin confirms that\na minimal sort of bundle size of ProseMirror is in the region of 200K, <a href=\"https://discuss.prosemirror.net/t/reduce-size-of-bundle/1705\">Marijn concurs</a>.\nTinyMCE and CKEditor don’t seem much different.</p>\n<p>In the meantime we run into a principled rant from a former mathematician and Medium Engineering lead Nick Santos\n<a href=\"https://medium.engineering/why-contenteditable-is-terrible-122d8a40e480\">Why ContentEditable is Terrible</a>. I’ve often\nreflected that perhaps the only benefit of my mathematical education is equipping me to tell anyone armed with an\nimpressive abstraction to eff off, but the analysis here seems sound and warns of pretty dreadful problems if we\nstart round-tripping stuff in earnest. Along the way we run into Steve Yegge’s salutory 2004 rant on the\n<a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/steveyegge2/nonesuch-beast\">Nonesuch Beast</a> which warns us that what we are trying to do\nis likely impossible — “People need to understand when they’re asking for something that’s going to be too complex to learn and use.”</p>\n<p>But still — we need to get off the ground more quickly and carry less\nweight with us for a while. Let’s put these warnings in our back pocket and look for something a bit more minimal.\nThis <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/webdev/comments/1dfq69v/looking_for_a_very_basic_wysiwyg_editor_in_js_to/\">Reddit thread</a>\ngives a few pointers but TipTap is itself based on ProseMirror. The AI suggestions for “rich text editor with smallest bundle size”\nbring up the hilariously named <a href=\"https://github.com/Alex-D/Trumbowyg\">Trumbowyg</a> which is sadly jQuery-based and then\nthe peculiarly promising <a href=\"https://github.com/jaredreich/pell/blob/master/src/pell.js\">Pell</a> which weighs in at an amazing\n145 lines. The <a href=\"https://codepen.io/meanbard/pen/rdjMQg\">live demo</a> seems perfectly reasonable, and when we look at the\nimplementation, we find that it is, surprise surprise, based wholly around <code>contentEditable</code>!</p>\n<p>This really does feel like something we can work with[^1], and morning noodlings suggest that contentEditable might not\nhave to be such a great unwashed. For a start, if we are tracking provenance of each rendered region to its source,\neach one might need to be editable (or read-only) in a different way anyway. We should research what the effects are\nof stringing together adjacent sibling nodes with <code>contentEditable</code> versus promoting up to the parent.</p>\n<p>In any case we have a workable lift-off vehicle – the entire (tiny) design can be Infusionised and first experiment\nis to check that it is perfectly workable to embed a self-hosted demo inside Infusion’s README file – it’s puzzling\nthat pell’s own demo takes the form of a little video.</p>\n<p>[Edit 2025-03-30:] This experiment failed, as one might have predicted it would. Of course we can’t inject arbitary JavaScript into\nGitHub’s rendering of the README markdown in its own interface. It appeared there might be a\n<a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/14951321/how-to-display-html-content-in-github-readme-md\">loophole</a> involving\nthe use of SVG files referencing a <code>foreignObject</code> holding arbitrary HTML but it seems that GitHub carefully renders\nany images in a <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/57857193/github-image-without-camo\">sandboxed environment</a>\nwhich are then hosted in a special domain <code>camo.githubusercontent.com</code>. We’ll have to host our experiment in a\nstandard GitHub page. Looks like there was a live demo on Jared’s personal Netlify website as recently as 2024.</p>\n<p>[^1] And to me the “pell” name is a pleasing signpost back to the great\n<a href=\"https://www.interaction-design.org/literature/author/pelle-ehn\">Pelle Ehn</a>\none of the founding fathers of malleable software thinking.</p>\n","summary":"The substrate’s transition to editability will need to present a “mostly rendered” view of itself – managed by some form of rich text editor. A huge “grab-bag list” is at JefMari’s curated list of Awesome Wysiwyg Editors which can rapidly be whittled down to a shortlist. CKEditor 5 and TinyMCE are familiar from decades ago. But the gold standard is clearly Marijn’s ProseMirror – basic example here and really nice markdown editing example here.","summaryText":"The substrate’s transition to editability will need to present a “mostly rendered” view of itself – managed by some form of rich text editor. A huge “grab-bag list” is at JefMari’s curated list of Awesome Wysiwyg Editors which can rapidly be whittled down to a shortlist. CKEditor 5 and TinyMCE are familiar from decades ago. But the gold standard is clearly Marijn’s ProseMirror – basic example here and really nice markdown editing example here.","truncated":true,"nextInSection":{"title":"Change of Tack on Templating","relPermalink":"/wip/2025-04-03-change-of-tack-on-templating/"}}],"shared":{"lang":"en","menu":[{"name":"Posts","url":"post","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"WiP","url":"wip","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"About","url":"page/about/","weight":3,"children":[]},{"name":"Docs","identifier":"docs","url":"docs","weight":3,"children":[{"name":"Fluid Signals","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-signals","weight":1,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid Integration Language","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-integration-language","weight":2,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid View","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-view","weight":3,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid PTF","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-ptf","weight":4,"children":[]},{"name":"Fluid tldraw integration","parent":"docs","url":"docs/fluid-tldraw","weight":5,"children":[]},{"name":"wend","parent":"docs","url":"docs/wend","weight":6,"children":[]}]},{"name":"Communities","url":"communities","weight":4,"children":[]},{"name":"Projects","url":"projects","weight":5,"children":[]},{"name":"Terms","url":"term","weight":5,"children":[]}],"buildYear":2026,"relatedIndex":[{"relPermalink":"/","title":"Antranig Basman","kind":"home","params":{}},{"relPermalink":"/post/","title":"post","kind":"section","params":{}},{"relPermalink":"/wip/","title":"wip","kind":"section","params":{}},{"relPermalink":"/wip/page/2/","title":"wip","kind":"section","params":